    {
      "id": "https://www.fastcompany.com/91626870/how-dropbox-wants-to-keep-ai-from-making-its-workers-less-intelligent",
      "url": "https://www.fastcompany.com/91626870/how-dropbox-wants-to-keep-ai-from-making-its-workers-less-intelligent",
      "title": "How Dropbox plans to prevent AI brain rot in its workers",
      "content_html": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Concerned about its workers <a href=\"https://www.fastcompany.com/91290531/science-shows-ai-is-probably-making-you-dumber-luckily-theres-a-fix\">losing brainpower as they outsource work</a> to <a href=\"https://www.fastcompany.com/section/artificial-intelligence\" title=\"AI\">artificial intelligence</a>, Dropbox is considering using cognitive training tools, like memory exercises and attention-span training, to keep them mentally sharp. </p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The cloud storage pioneer has also experimented with “analog” offsites, where executives practice breathwork and put aside their electronic devices. </p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“We’re incredibly optimistic about [AI’s] impact, but we also believe we have a responsibility to understand how it’s affecting the people who use it every day,” Melanie Rosenwasser, the cloud storage pioneer’s chief people officer, told <em>Fast Company.</em>  </p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“Brain rot” has emerged as a term to describe the cognitive decline caused by AI and social media, <a href=\"https://www.nytimes.com/2025/11/06/technology/personaltech/ai-social-media-brain-rot.html\">as studies have shown</a>. “That’s why we’re beginning to explore training around brain health and cognitive well-being, while also experimenting with ways to foster critical thinking and human connection,” said Rosenwasser.</p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">While <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27697851/\">evidence is limited</a> that brain-training exercises improve general intelligence, some research suggests that targeted exercises can produce lasting improvements in specific mental skills. </p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A National Institutes of Health-funded study found that training in reasoning and processing speed, for example, <a href=\"https://www.nia.nih.gov/news/cognitive-training-shows-staying-power\">produced benefits</a> that lasted up to a decade in older adults.</p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“We’re approaching this with curiosity and humility, recognizing that this is an emerging area where there is still a lot to learn,” Rosenwasser said. </p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Dropbox would not be the first company to provide such exercises to workers. </p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Last year, commercial real estate giant JLL set up a “brain gym” in its Singapore office featuring “lighted pods” and “vision training boards.” Workers who participated reported better focus and fewer distractions, according to <a href=\"https://www.businessinsider.com/americas-obsession-paying-to-get-smarter-brain-hacking-adhd-alzheimers-2026-1\"><em>Business Insider</em></a><em>. </em></p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In 2024, another <a href=\"https://centerforbrainhealth.org/article/fischer-case-study\">commercial real estate firm</a>, Fischer, partnered with the University of Texas at Dallas on a three-month trial program intended to “improve cognitive performance and support better work habits.” </p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The workers showed measurable progress on a composite index measuring “cognitive capacity, sense of well-being, quality of social interactions and complexity of daily routines,” researchers said.</p>",
      "date_published": "2026-10-25T07:00:00+00:00",
